1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,383
A new home kitchen near Mrs.

2
00:00:02,383 --> 00:00:06,039
Rao starts selling millet
laddoos too, at the same price.

3
00:00:06,339 --> 00:00:07,239
Yet Mrs.

4
00:00:07,118 --> 00:00:08,991
Rao stays with Laddoo Lab.

5
00:00:08,991 --> 00:00:09,891
Why?

6
00:00:09,857 --> 00:00:12,210
Because she is buying
much more than a laddoo.

7
00:00:12,860 --> 00:00:17,387
Zoya draws the six-pack as rings,
one inside another, like an onion.

8
00:00:17,737 --> 00:00:20,244
At the very centre is the reason Mrs.

9
00:00:20,244 --> 00:00:24,393
Rao buys at all: a healthy treat
her daughter will actually eat.

10
00:00:24,743 --> 00:00:26,891
Everything else is wrapped around that.

11
00:00:27,541 --> 00:00:29,996
These rings are called product levels.

12
00:00:30,296 --> 00:00:33,864
Philip Kotler describes five
of them, from the core outward.

13
00:00:34,215 --> 00:00:36,844
And Theodore Levitt put
the big idea simply.

14
00:00:36,844 --> 00:00:40,873
Firms compete less on what they make,
and more on what they add around

15
00:00:40,873 --> 00:00:41,773
it.

16
00:00:41,936 --> 00:00:44,421
Let's build the six-pack, ring by ring.

17
00:00:44,771 --> 00:00:45,796
Core benefit.

18
00:00:45,796 --> 00:00:49,667
What she really buys: a healthy
treat her daughter enjoys.

19
00:00:50,017 --> 00:00:51,095
Basic product.

20
00:00:51,095 --> 00:00:53,675
The thing itself: six millet laddoos.

21
00:00:54,025 --> 00:00:55,174
Expected product.

22
00:00:55,174 --> 00:00:59,758
What every buyer takes for granted:
fresh, clean, sealed, and on time.

23
00:01:00,108 --> 00:01:01,397
Augmented product.

24
00:01:01,397 --> 00:01:06,351
The extras that beat rivals: Monday
delivery, a photo message, and a little

25
00:01:06,351 --> 00:01:08,443
card from Nani in every pack.

26
00:01:08,793 --> 00:01:09,938
Potential product.

27
00:01:09,938 --> 00:01:14,447
What it could become one day: a tiffin
plan with a new flavour every month.

28
00:01:14,797 --> 00:01:17,446
The new kitchen matches the inner rings.

29
00:01:17,446 --> 00:01:19,798
Laddoo Lab wins on the outer ones.

30
00:01:20,448 --> 00:01:22,118
Rivals can copy the core.

31
00:01:22,118 --> 00:01:24,178
You win on the rings around it.

32
00:01:24,878 --> 00:01:25,897
Now zoom out.

33
00:01:25,897 --> 00:01:28,615
Where does one pack
sit among all products?

34
00:01:28,965 --> 00:01:31,460
Kotler calls this the product hierarchy.

35
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:34,472
Six steps, from a big
need down to one item.

36
00:01:34,822 --> 00:01:35,769
Need family.

37
00:01:35,769 --> 00:01:37,869
The deep need: healthy eating.

38
00:01:38,169 --> 00:01:39,224
Product family.

39
00:01:39,224 --> 00:01:42,115
Everything that meets
it, like healthy snacks.

40
00:01:42,415 --> 00:01:43,447
Product class.

41
00:01:43,447 --> 00:01:46,070
A group inside that, like sweet snacks.

42
00:01:46,370 --> 00:01:47,520
Product line.

43
00:01:47,520 --> 00:01:49,133
Laddoo Lab's laddoos.

44
00:01:49,433 --> 00:01:50,516
Product type.

45
00:01:50,516 --> 00:01:52,311
Millet laddoos in packs.

46
00:01:52,611 --> 00:01:53,511
Item.

47
00:01:53,204 --> 00:01:58,047
One exact version: the ragi six-pack,
at a hundred and twenty rupees.

48
00:01:58,697 --> 00:02:01,428
Where do we see product
levels at work today?

49
00:02:01,778 --> 00:02:02,867
Core benefit.

50
00:02:02,867 --> 00:02:05,502
A hotel really sells rest and sleep.

51
00:02:05,852 --> 00:02:07,156
Expected product.

52
00:02:07,156 --> 00:02:10,233
Guests expect a clean
bed and fresh towels.

53
00:02:10,583 --> 00:02:11,804
Augmented product.

54
00:02:11,804 --> 00:02:15,321
A fast check-in and a free
airport ride bring them back.

55
00:02:15,671 --> 00:02:16,967
Potential product.

56
00:02:16,967 --> 00:02:20,538
A phone maker plans the
features of next year's model.

57
00:02:21,188 --> 00:02:23,029
But adding rings has a cost.

58
00:02:23,379 --> 00:02:24,426
The good side.

59
00:02:24,426 --> 00:02:27,283
Outer rings set you
apart, and build loyalty.

60
00:02:27,633 --> 00:02:28,901
The hard side.

61
00:02:28,901 --> 00:02:30,797
Every extra costs money.

62
00:02:30,797 --> 00:02:34,118
And today's extra becomes
tomorrow's expectation.

63
00:02:34,468 --> 00:02:37,216
After six months of photo messages, Mrs.

64
00:02:37,216 --> 00:02:40,380
Rao will complain the one
Monday it doesn't come.

65
00:02:40,730 --> 00:02:42,115
Nani has the answer.

66
00:02:42,115 --> 00:02:44,559
Never let the extras hide a weak core.

67
00:02:44,909 --> 00:02:45,821
The lesson?

68
00:02:45,821 --> 00:02:50,042
Protect the core first, then add
only what customers truly value.

69
00:02:50,692 --> 00:02:51,627
Quick check.

70
00:02:51,627 --> 00:02:55,050
Monday delivery, and a card
from Nani in every pack.

71
00:02:55,050 --> 00:02:56,930
Which product level is this?

72
00:02:58,830 --> 00:03:00,147
Augmented product!

73
00:03:00,147 --> 00:03:02,967
Extras that go beyond what buyers expect.

74
00:03:03,567 --> 00:03:04,580
So, remember.

75
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:09,668
A product has five levels, from the core
benefit out to the potential product.

76
00:03:10,018 --> 00:03:11,654
Rivals can copy the core.

77
00:03:11,654 --> 00:03:13,952
You win with what you add around it.

78
00:03:14,302 --> 00:03:19,022
And the product hierarchy runs from
a big need down to one exact item.

79
00:03:19,622 --> 00:03:24,074
Next time, we'll follow a product from
birth to old age: the product life

80
00:03:24,074 --> 00:03:24,974
cycle.
